<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">PLATFORM ARCHITECTURAL PHILOSOPHY</div>
  <p>ORCA is an enterprise-grade, life-safety marine intelligence platform. It bridges satellite oceanography (ISRO Oceansat, MODIS), real-time buoy telemetry (INCOIS), and astronomical tidal harmonics (Survey of India) with an agentic AI copilot. <strong>Crucially, probabilistic AI models (LLMs) are strictly prohibited from calculating navigational physics or risk scores; all life-critical routing and safety evaluations are performed by 100% deterministic mathematical engines.</strong></p>
</div>

---

<h2>1. High-Level System Architecture & Flow</h2>
<p>The platform operates across four decoupled, interconnected tiers:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 18%;">Layer</th>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Core Technology</th>
      <th style="width: 57%;">Architectural Responsibilities</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>1. Client Tier (Frontend)</strong></td>
      <td>React 19, Vite, Leaflet GIS, Vanilla CSS Design System</td>
      <td>Renders responsive coastal intelligence HUD, Leaflet maps, high-contrast dark/light theme, GPS location resolver, tri-lingual localization (EN/HI/MR), and lifted multi-tab chat drawer.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>2. API & Gateway Tier</strong></td>
      <td>FastAPI, Uvicorn (ASGI), Python 3.13, Pydantic V2</td>
      <td>Asynchronous REST endpoints, static bundle hosting, CORS middleware, session memory caching, and sub-second payload routing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>3. Multi-Agent AI Tier</strong></td>
      <td>Hierarchical Orchestrator + 6 Specialist Engines</td>
      <td>NLU parsing, great-circle geodesy, A* risk pathfinding, ray-casting geofencing, 5-factor risk scoring, and Gemini Flash-Lite explanation synthesis.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>4. Data & Cloud Tier</strong></td>
      <td>Supabase PostgreSQL, PostGIS, JWT Auth</td>
      <td>Relational tables for PFZs, coastal alerts, wave observations, tidal models, and indexed conversation history vectors.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

---

<h2>2. Complete End-to-End User & Data Workflow</h2>

<div class="workflow-step">
  <h4 style="color: #0369a1; margin-top: 0;">Step 1: Access Control & Compulsory Authentication Gate</h4>
  <p><strong>Trigger:</strong> User opens the application in any web browser at <code>http://localhost:8001</code>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Client Execution:</strong> <code>App.jsx</code> inspects <code>currentUser</code> state from <code>localStorage.getItem('orca_user')</code> and validates with Supabase <code>getSession()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Unauthenticated Interception:</strong> If no authenticated user exists, the application locks the workspace shell and displays a non-dismissible <code>AuthModal</code> with <code>12px</code> backdrop blur, language switcher, and authentication options (Email/Password login, Signup, or instant Captain/Researcher Demo sign-in).</li>
    <li><strong>Session Unlock:</strong> Upon successful authentication, user JWT tokens and metadata are saved to <code>localStorage</code>, unlocking full portal navigation.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="workflow-step">
  <h4 style="color: #0369a1; margin-top: 0;">Step 2: Automated Geolocation & Coastal Hub Resolution</h4>
  <p><strong>Trigger:</strong> Dashboard initializes or user clicks "Allow Location".</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Browser API:</strong> <code>navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition()</code> acquires GPS coordinates (e.g., Lat: 18.94, Lon: 72.83).</li>
    <li><strong>Backend Resolution:</strong> Coordinates are sent to <code>/api/location/resolve</code>. The GIS engine calculates great-circle distance to the nearest Indian fishing harbor (e.g., Sassoon Dock, Mumbai) using <code>indian_coastal_registry.py</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>State Synchronization:</strong> The entire portal dynamically switches to this port context: loading port-specific SST, wave heights, and tidal schedules.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="workflow-step">
  <h4 style="color: #0369a1; margin-top: 0;">Step 3: Multi-Source Marine Data Aggregation</h4>
  <p><strong>Trigger:</strong> Client triggers parallel asynchronous fetches for the active port sector.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>GET /api/analytics</code>: Retrieves 7-day SST gradients, Chlorophyll-a concentration, wave height trends, and official Survey of India tidal harmonic predictions.</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/pfz?port_id=mumbai</code>: Retrieves active satellite PFZs within 80km, ranked by confidence and depth.</li>
    <li><code>GET /api/alerts?port_id=mumbai</code>: Fetches severity-sorted bulletins (Critical, Caution, Coastal bulletins).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="workflow-step">
  <h4 style="color: #0369a1; margin-top: 0;">Step 4: AI Conversational Copilot Query Execution</h4>
  <p><strong>Trigger:</strong> Captain types or speaks a marine query (e.g., <em>"उद्या सकाळी मुंबईजवळ मासेमारीसाठी सुरक्षित क्षेत्र कोणते आहे?"</em>).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stage 1 (NLU Agent):</strong> Classifies intent (<code>TRIP_PLANNING</code>), extracts language (<code>mr</code>), location (<code>Mumbai</code>), and time window (<code>Tomorrow Morning</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Stage 2 (Orchestrator):</strong> Dispatches tasks concurrently to Marine, Weather, and GIS agents.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage 3 (Marine Agent):</strong> Identifies candidate zones (e.g., <code>PFZ-MUM-01</code>, 42 km offshore, SST 28.1&deg;C, Chl 1.36 mg/m&sup3;).</li>
    <li><strong>Stage 4 (Weather Agent):</strong> Pulls forecasted wave swell (1.2m) and wind velocity (16 km/h).</li>
    <li><strong>Stage 5 (GIS Agent):</strong> Calculates Haversine nautical distance (22.8 nm), compass bearing (248.8&deg; WSW), and runs Jordan Curve ray-casting to ensure the corridor avoids naval firing ranges and international boundaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage 6 (Risk Engine):</strong> Applies deterministic 5-factor mathematical weighting: Risk Score = 17.0 (Band: <strong>LOW RISK</strong>). Checks tidal schedule to recommend high-tide departure at 04:12 AM.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage 7 (Decision Agent):</strong> Gemini Flash-Lite synthesizes numerical evidence into pure Marathi markdown briefing with structured cards and action buttons (<em>"View Route on Map"</em>).</li>
    <li><strong>Database Persistence:</strong> Both user query and assistant response are persisted to Supabase <code>chat_messages</code> and local cache, enabling instant multi-tab drawer recall.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

